export const LESSONS={"weights": {"title": "Weight the same month.", "objective": "CH6-01 · Compute a long-only two-asset return from beginning-of-month weights.", "body": "<p>A portfolio combines holdings. Its one-period simple return is the sum of each beginning-of-period weight times the corresponding same-period asset return, when there are no external flows or costs. This chapter uses two assets, no borrowing, and weights between 0 and 1 summing to 1.</p><div class=\"formula\">Portfolio return (%) = wA × rA + (1−wA) × rB</div><p>Worked example: start 100 with 60 in A and 40 in B. A gains 10%, B loses 5%. Endings 66 and 38 total 104, so portfolio return 4%. The weighted calculation .6×10+.4×(−5)=4% agrees. Using 60 rather than.6 would inflate the answer 100 fold.</p><p>Weights describe the <strong>start</strong> of the measured month. Ending weights have already moved with returns; substituting them would use information from later in the period. Match asset months, currency, distribution and fee basis before weighting. A missing month is not automatically a 0% return.</p><p><strong>Predict:</strong> is a weighted return always between the two asset returns? For these long-only weights summing to 1, yes. That check can catch arithmetic mistakes. It need not hold with leverage or short positions, which are outside this prototype.</p>", "reflection": "Verify the weighted calculation from holdings, identify beginning-of-period weights and explain the date alignment requirement.", "criteria": "I used weights as fractions summing to one and paired same-date returns without look-ahead."}, "diversification": {"title": "Two assets are not a promise.", "objective": "CH6-02 · Distinguish allocation concentration from sample return diversification.", "body": "<p><strong>Diversification</strong> spreads exposures, but counting names does not establish independent risks. Assets that move identically produce the same return path regardless of how these long-only weights are split. Sample correlation helps describe joint movement; future correlation can change.</p><div class=\"formula\">Allocation concentration H = wA² + wB²<br>Sample portfolio SD = SD of the actual weighted monthly returns</div><p>Worked concentration:50/50 gives .5²+.5²=.5;80/20 gives .8²+.2²=.68;100/0 gives 1. For exactly two long-only assets the minimum is.5. Lower H means more even allocation <em>across these two names</em>; it is not a complete measure of economic exposure, liquidity, downside or common-factor risk.</p><p>Worked return paths: A=[10,−10]%, B=[−10,10]%. Rebalanced 50/50 returns[0,0]%, while either asset fluctuates. Change B to[10,−10]% and the 50/50 return remains[10,−10]%; splitting names alone no longer reduces sample SD. Diversification depends on paired returns and assumptions, not an automatic reward for adding a holding.</p><p><strong>Predict:</strong> can concentration fall while portfolio SD rises? Yes, if the added asset is more volatile or co-movement changes. Examine both. This six-row invented sample demonstrates a mechanism; it does not identify a recommended allocation.</p>", "reflection": "Calculate H and explain what it omits. Use the identical-return counterexample to qualify a diversification claim.", "criteria": "I distinguished allocation balance from actual sample return spread and future diversification."}, "rebalance": {"title": "Choose a policy before comparing.", "objective": "CH6-03 · Compare monthly constant-mix rebalancing with buy-and-hold weight drift.", "body": "<p><strong>Buy-and-hold</strong> keeps initial asset units unchanged: each sleeve compounds separately and weights drift. <strong>Monthly constant-mix</strong> resets weights to the declared target at each month boundary, before the next month’s return. The simulation trades after the completed month and before the following one, using only then-known holdings and the fixed target.</p><div class=\"formula\">Constant mix: Wt = Wt−1 × [1 + (wA rAt + wB rBt)/100]<br>Buy-and-hold: At = At−1 × (1+rAt/100); Bt similarly<br>Drifted A weight = At / (At+Bt)</div><p>Worked start 100 split 50/50, A returns[20,−20]%, B[0,0]%. Buy-and-hold sleeves 50→60→48 and 50→50→50 end 98. After month 1 its A weight is 60/110≈.545455. Constant mix: month 1 return 10%, wealth 110; reset 55/55; month 2 return−10%, wealth 99.</p><p>This path favours constant mix by 1 currency unit. Counterexample A[20,20]%, B[0,0]%: buy-and-hold ends 122; constant mix ends 121. Rebalancing does not always improve ending wealth.</p><p>Costs, bid–ask spreads, slippage, taxes, dividends as separate cash, borrowing and liquidity constraints are omitted. All inputs are total returns and no new money enters. Rebalancing can incur turnover and costs; a difference before costs is not a tradable net advantage. Our chart compares the stated two policies on identical dates, never an optimal strategy.</p>", "reflection": "Trace both policies from holdings, explain when resetting occurs, and show why either policy can win before omitted costs.", "criteria": "I separated weight drift from reset weights, used only prior-known policy information and stated missing frictions."}, "beta": {"title": "Read the slope, preserve the pairs.", "objective": "CH6-04 · Interpret sample correlation and beta against an aligned benchmark, including undefined cases.", "body": "<p><strong>Beta</strong> is the sample slope of portfolio returns against benchmark returns when an intercept is included. It describes linear sensitivity in the supplied paired observations. Compute sample covariance divided by sample benchmark variance. The n−1 factors cancel in the ratio.</p><div class=\"formula\">Beta = Σ[(portfolio−mean portfolio)(market−mean market)]<br>÷ Σ(market−mean market)²</div><p>Worked aligned market=[−1,0,1]%, portfolio=[−2,0,2]%: cross-product sum 4, market squared sum 2, beta 2. Portfolio moves twice as far along this exact sample line. Correlation is 1, not 2: correlation describes standardised association; beta also reflects relative scale.</p><p>If the benchmark is constant, variance=0 and beta is <strong>undefined</strong>. A constant portfolio with a varying benchmark has beta 0, but its correlation is undefined because portfolio SD=0. Distinguish those cases. The plot in the lab puts benchmark returns on the horizontal axis and portfolio returns on the vertical, and provides the exact table.</p><p><strong>Predict:</strong> does beta 2 mean the next market gain 1% guarantees a portfolio gain 2%? No. A fitted sample slope does not remove the intercept, residual variation, estimation error or regime change. It is neither causation nor a complete risk score. Different benchmark definitions and dates can change beta.</p>", "reflection": "Explain the slope and its units, distinguish beta from correlation, and identify zero-variance and predictive limits.", "criteria": "I preserved matching dates, used benchmark variance in the denominator and rejected a guaranteed next-period interpretation."}};
export const SOURCES=[["SEC Investor.gov: asset allocation and diversification", "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/asset-allocation"], ["J.P. Morgan Asset Management: beta glossary definition (covariance / benchmark variance)", "https://am.jpmorgan.com/hk/en/asset-management/adv/tools-resources/investment-glossary/"]];
